Date: August 7, 2026 
Tools used: Router admin web interface, browser 
Environment: My own home network (authorized)

Objective

While investigating my home network's overall security posture (following up on earlier Nmap recon of connected devices), I checked whether my router's admin panel was still using its factory-default credentials.

Steps taken
Identified the router's local admin IP (commonly 192.168.1.1 — confirmed via ipconfig/ip route gateway address).
Navigated to the admin login page in a browser.
Attempted login using the manufacturer's published default username/password (publicly available in the router's documentation/manual).
Confirmed access was granted — the router was still running factory-default credentials.
Changed the admin password to a long, unique, non-default value.
Checked and confirmed remote/WAN management was disabled, limiting admin access to the local network only.

What I found
The router's administrative interface was still protected only by its factory-default username and password. These credentials are publicly documented by the manufacturer and are not a meaningful security control on their own — anyone with local network access (or remote access, if WAN management were enabled) could have logged in with no effort.

Why it matters
Default credentials are one of the most commonly exploited weaknesses in consumer networking equipment, precisely because they aren't secret — they're published and searchable. A router is a uniquely high-value target compared to other devices on a network because it sits at the center of all traffic and controls network-wide settings. Specific risks this exposed me to included: Full administrative takeover of the router, DNS hijacking, Firewall/port manipulation, Botnet recruitment, Network-wide lateral movement.

Remediation applied
Changed the router admin password to a strong, unique credential not used anywhere else.
Verified remote/WAN administrative access was disabled, restricting admin login to the local network only.

Next steps
Periodically audit router settings as part of routine home network hygiene, not a one-time fix.
Apply the same default-credential check to any other network-connected devices (smart TV, printer, IoT devices) discovered during earlier Nmap scans.
Consider enabling the router's guest/IoT network feature to segment less-trusted devices from primary devices going forward.

Related exam/job-relevant concepts
Domain 2 (Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations — default configurations, common vulnerability classes), Domain 3 (Security Architecture — the router as central network infrastructure), Domain 5 (Security Program Management — routine security auditing)
